    title: "Ethiopia's army says it has repelled attacks in first comment on fresh fighting",
    summary: "The military says its soldiers inflicted casualties as fears grow of a resumption of a full-blown civil war.",
    preview: "Ethiopia's army says it has repelled attacks from Tigrayan forces and their allies in parts of the north of the country, in its first statement since fighting broke out on Wednesday. The army said hostilities began in an area of Amhara region close to the border with Tigray, adding that its soldiers had inflicted heavy casualties and seized weapons. The developments come as tensions between the federal government and Tigrayan leaders have sharply escalated over the last few days, raising fears that a devastating civil war could resume.",
    body: "Ethiopia's army says it has repelled attacks from Tigrayan forces and their allies in parts of the north of the country, in its first statement since fighting broke out on Wednesday.\n\nThe army said hostilities began in an area of Amhara region close to the border with Tigray, adding that its soldiers had inflicted heavy casualties and seized weapons.\n\nThe developments come as tensions between the federal government and Tigrayan leaders have sharply escalated over the last few days, raising fears that a devastating civil war could resume.\n\nOn Wednesday, the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF), which dominates the region, seized control of three airports from federal police.\n\nThe TPLF has not commented on the army's claims and the BBC has not been able to independently verify the army's claims.\n\nThe BBC is unable to report from Tigray and surrounding areas.\n\nThere are unconfirmed reports that TPLF forces have launched attacks in the neighbouring regions of Afar and Amhara.\n\nA humanitarian source in Afar told AFP news agency on Thursday that Tigrayan forces had launched heavy artillery attacks as they attempted to take control of the town of Yallo, near the border with Tigray.\n\n\"We are in the situation of full-blown war now,\" TPLF vice-president Amanuel Assefa is quoted by the news agency as saying.\n\nThe latest escalation follows months of rising tensions between Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed's government and Tigray authorities, who fought a devastating war from 2020 to 2022.\n\nThat ended in an African Union-brokered peace deal but over recent months both sides have been accusing each other of violating the agreement.\n\nIt was one of the most deadly conflicts this century - the AU's mediator estimated some 600,000 people died as the fighting drove the region to the precipice of famine.\n\nOn Sunday, the TPLF announced an alliance with six other armed groups to oust the government.\n\nFollowing the seizure of the airports, Ethiopian Airlines has cancelled all flights to Tigray.\n\nResidents of Tigray's main city of Mekelle told the BBC that local forces had taken over control of the city's airport from the national police early on Wednesday.\n\nThis followed reports of drone strikes in Tigray in recent days.\n\nA Mekelle resident told the BBC's Newsday programme on Thursday there had been no clashes in the city, unlike in the previous war, with most fighting happening elsewhere.\n\n\"Mekelle is calm, except the stress from the people,\" said the resident, only identified as Fitsum for his safety. He added that the biggest problem was economic, with people not having enough cash.\n\n\"People are in a mood where they are distressed because it's been so difficult, so tough. The last seven or eight months the government has been denying budgets to the civil servants and all the banks were running out of cash.\"\n\nFitsum said people had been relying on mobile banking but with the risk of communications being cut off, \"people are going to be in grave danger because there is almost no cash\".\n\nWith the outbreak of war, people were limiting their movements and the city was all quiet - \"it's like a curfew has been placed,\" he told the BBC.\n\nMore about Tigray from the BBC:\n\n- Ethiopia PM's party wins landslide as fears grow of new conflict\n\n- Published21 June\n\n- Is Ethiopia heading back to war in Tigray?\n\n- Published26 February\n\n- 'I lost my leg on the way home from school'\n\n- Published2 November 2024\n\nGo to BBCAfrica.com, external for more news from the African continent.\n\nFollow us on Twitter @BBCAfrica, external, on Facebook at BBC Africa, external or on Instagram at bbcafrica, external",
